findings.problem:
Hitstop is set in exactly one place: `plugFlash()` sets `this.hitstop=.09` (src/game.ts:249). Its only caller is the cable seating in Game.action('cable') (game.ts:205). frame() honours it (game.ts:456). A grep for hitstop, timeScale and slow finds nothing else: no time.scale and no slow-mo anywhere.

These moments get no hitstop:
- the job win, Game.win() (game.ts:350);
- the Lunch fail beat (src/lunch-runtime.ts:285);
- Meeting and Lunch breaker trips (src/meeting-strip.ts:82, lunch-runtime.ts:264);
- the Lunch water short and scorch (lunch-runtime.ts:264-265);
- the arcade fuse trip, block pop and LED pop (arcade/station.ts:288-311);
- the garage dead-short fuse (garage/station.ts:237);
- every bench sign-off;
- the slingshot release (game.ts:256);
- Lunch plug-ins: lunch-runtime.ts:156 calls `g.audio.plug()` without `plugFlash()`, so Lunch's 9 cable connections get neither the freeze nor the flash/ring/star burst that Playground and Meeting get.

DR-11 requires 'a short hitstop' at success and failure moments. Today the freeze lands on the least important beat (a plug that will be followed by a switch press) and never on the beats that matter. ?manual skips the frame loop entirely (game.ts:457), so no spec can even observe hitstop.

The Lunch fail path reuses `g.won=true` to stop play (src/lunch-runtime.ts:285). PipRig keys its celebration pose off `won`:
- the bounce at src/render/pipRig.ts:57;
- both arms thrown overhead and waving at :77;
- the head tilted up at :88;
- Game turns Pip to face the camera at src/game.ts:407.

So at the moment lunch spoils, Pip cheers with arms up in the power room. Screenshot juice-audio-12 shows it: arms raised and bouncing, the failed fridge a small icon far up-frame. The groans say failure; Pip's body says success.

winAt is set only in win() (game.ts:350), so the push-in camera (game.ts:369) doesn't run for a fail. The cause (the fridge at (−5.3,−2.1), or the oven) stays wherever it happened to be, often off-screen. The DR-11 framing rule (keep Pip in frame with the machine) is broken in the fail case. DR-28 (the cause shown in the world first) is weakened.

findings.fix:
On a send-back:
- play a rubber-stamp thunk (lowpass noise 400 at .1) plus a reject buzzer (square 140 Hz, two 0.12 s pulses at .07);
- animate the board sliding off toward the tester and dropping back onto the table (reuse `dropIn()`) with a clatter;
- flash the problem rings red once (120 ms) and add a .06 s hitstop.

findings.problem:
At sign-off (arcade/station.ts:271-278), the station records the verdict, fires cheer(), then on the same call:
- `index++`, powerOff();
- loads the next job's start setup;
- runs showJob().

juice-audio-15 was taken immediately after the arcade sign-off. The bench already shows cabinet 2 (RHYTHM, 3.3 V, 47 Ω), and the only trace of success is a small toast, 'Pinball bumper: Elegant ★'. The confetti goes off behind the table, out of the bench camera (shared.ts:11-15, which correctly fixes A10). The repaired cabinet lights up (`room.light`) off-screen.

The reward for each solved puzzle is invisible at the camera the player is using. The same immediate swap happens in the garage (station.ts:229), QFN (qfn:287) and observatory/spectrum (jobIndex++).

findings.fix:
Add a 1.0–1.2 s 'served' beat before loading the next job:
- keep the solved board lit, with the LED or load glowing and a green 'PASS ★' stamp on the monitor;
- bounce the tier medal in the station panel;
- add a .05 s hitstop;
- then slide the next board in.

In the arcade, briefly cut or pan to the cabinet lighting up (`room.light`), or show a picture-in-picture of it on the bench monitor.

findings.problem:
`knock()` fires whenever the cable's bend count changes, both when a wrap is added and when it is removed (src/game.ts:284). Bends flicker as the rope settles, so the hooked logs show long runs of knocks:
- Playground [slingshot aftermath]: 10 knocks in 1.5 s;
- Meeting [walk] carrying the extended reel: 9 knocks in one leg;
- [coupler F]: 5 knocks.

The knock was meant to say 'the cable caught a corner', a key routing cue (DR-46). As a constant patter it carries no information and masks the strain creak.
